<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Nan of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 314–256 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Nan of Zhou was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, reigning from 314 to 256 BCE. By his time royal power was nominal, and he lived under the protection of the Duchy of Western Zhou. In 256 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> took the royal domain and King Nan died, ending some eight centuries of Zhou kingship.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>King Nan, personal name Ji Yan and less often called King Yin, was the son of King Shenjing and grandson of King Xian, and the 37th king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. His reign of fifty-nine years was the longest of the dynasty and of all pre-imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. By then even the remaining crown land was split between two rival lords: Western Zhou, which held the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>, and Eastern Zhou, centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Chengzhou</a>. How much ritual standing he kept is disputed: the feudal states took royal titles for themselves, yet inscriptions and some accounts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> suggest he was still respected as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At the start of his reign he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Chengzhou</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>. Foreign powers then invaded the royal lands repeatedly, beginning with the attack of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> on Yiyang in 307 BCE. The two Zhou courts survived through shifting alliances and schemes, such as the plan of the Scribe Yan to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> suspect Chu while Han ceded land to Zhou. Summoned to the Qin court to debate an attack on the Han city of Nanyang, Nan instead worked with Han to block the road and avoid the war.</p><p>The lords of Western and Eastern Zhou also fought each other. Han, nominally allied to Western Zhou, looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a> and the royal palace. In 273 BCE Ma Fan enlisted King Anxi of Wei to build a fortification wall that would protect the Nine Tripod Cauldrons, the emblems of royal authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> spared the king's land for a time, its rulers believing that destroying the domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a> would harm their name, and Nan and his officials even reported to Qin on the armies of Han, Wei and Zhao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> occupied the Han town of Fushu near the old capital, Nan and Western Zhou joined an alliance against it, and Western Zhou's attack was driven back. Nan travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin">King Zhaoxiang of Qin</a>, was made Duke of West Zhou, and died soon after while held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>. Qin took the Nine Tripod Cauldrons and exiled his heir Ji Wen to Danhu. In Eastern Zhou, Duke Wen was proclaimed king but recognised nowhere else; he and Nan's son Ji Zhao were defeated in 251 and 249 BCE. The Qin scholars of the Lüshi Chunqiu wrote that there was no greater turmoil than the absence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a>, a title later taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Nan of Zhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>King Nan</b> lived under the protection of Western Zhou, and the duchy survived by playing off the great powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-western-zhou">Duchy of Western Zhou</a></li><li><blockquote>In 256 BCE the State of Qin seized Wangcheng and <b>King Nan</b>, the last Zhou king, died.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-zhou">Eastern Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Wangcheng, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.67,112.42&amp;year=-285" aria-label="Wangcheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of King Nan of Zhou"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4468 4584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4468 4584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.67,112.42&amp;year=-285">What was here in 285 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
